  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/available_again_pterobrycon_myrnae_the_semaphore_tetra_en/",
      "html": "<p>A spectacular, unproblematic and peaceful tetra with internal fertilisation. Females reach up to 55 mm total length; males stay 10–15 mm smaller.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/available_again_pterobrycon_myrnae_the_semaphore_tetra_en/",
      "html": "<p>This fish is restricted to fast running brooks in Costa Rica.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/available_again_pterobrycon_myrnae_the_semaphore_tetra_en/",
      "html": "<p>It is peaceful. The mating rituals are very interesting to observe; the male presents the \"semaphore scales\" widely spread.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/available_again_pterobrycon_myrnae_the_semaphore_tetra_en/",
      "html": "<p>The fish has an extreme sexual dimorphism. Females are plain blue-grey, whereas the males are particularly colourful. Only the males develop the semaphore-like long scales which are responsible for the common name. Moreover the males have longer anal and ventral fins. The females become slightly bigger (up to 55 mm total length) than the males, which stay 10–15 mm smaller. The sexes are distinguishable only when the fish are six months old.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/available_again_pterobrycon_myrnae_the_semaphore_tetra_en/",
      "html": "<p>The species has internal fertilisation and the female is able to store sperm over a long period. The eggs are attached to substrates by the female. Some offspring have been produced from wild-collected fish, and work on the F1 generation is ongoing.</p>"
    }
  ],
